[Music]  i want to say a happy new year to you  all and that i'm looking forward to 2022  being a positive and productive year for  the isle of planet  here's a quick round up of projects that  will be coming to fruition in the next  12 months  the margate town deal is gaining  momentum  we submitted the first business case to  the government in october for a 6.9  million pound project to support  creative land trust  this will allow the council to acquire  unused properties and transform them  into affordable commercial spaces for  creative and support services  a new chair mr brian halton from margate  has been appointed  aside from being a local brian has a lot  of development experience in both the  private and public sectors  there's also a community exhibition on  saturday the 26th of february where  people can come along and learn more  about the projects and give their  feedback  in other regeneration news leveling up  fun projects for both ramsgate and  margate are already underway  workers started on the former marks and  spencer's building in margate high  street which will be the home of the new  margate digital project  at the moment we're removing asbestos  and renovating the roof of the building  and work should be complete by the  spring  in ramsgate we have resubmitted our  marine license application for burst 45  in the port so it can run alongside our  outline planning application  we are still intending to start  construction in may of this year and  people will have the opportunity to  comment on both applications as they are  both subject to a consultation period  we've also been busy updating thanit's  local plan  and over the last six weeks residents  have been commenting on the supporting  evidence that we will be using to  publish a draft of our updated plan  there is an explainer video on all of  our social media channels featuring  voiceovers from both myself and bob  porter our director of housing and  planning to help bring this process to  life  council officers have been hard at work  securing funding to enable us to help  our residents  our housing option team has been awarded  175 000 pounds by the kent housing group  to help us support local tenants and  landlords within the private sector who  may be struggling to pay their rent or  mortgages as a result of covid 19.  our private sector housing team has  secured over 76 000 pounds of funding  from the government for a project to  improve energy efficiency standards in  privately rented properties  which would especially help our  residents now during the current energy  price crisis  i'm pleased to announce that the council  can now present a balanced budget for  the financial year ahead a significant  improvement to the 691 000 deficit that  was predicted in november of last year  net service spending is increasing by  1.182 million pounds a 7 increase on  last year resulting in no service cuts  we have also managed to contribute over  six hundred thousand pounds to our  reserves further consolidating our  financial stability of the council  finally i would like to thank our  uniformed service team  many of whom continue to work right  across the christmas period to ensure  minimal disruption to waste collection  services around the  district you

